## 4. Linux File System Hierarchy

Linux uses a hierarchical file system.

The root directory `/` is the starting point of the entire directory structure.

### Important Directories

| Directory | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `/` | Root directory |
| `/home` | User home directories |
| `/root` | Root user's home directory |
| `/etc` | Configuration files |
| `/var` | Logs and variable data |
| `/var/log` | System and application logs |
| `/bin` | Essential user commands |
| `/sbin` | System administration commands |
| `/usr` | User applications and shared resources |
| `/tmp` | Temporary files |
| `/dev` | Device files |
| `/proc` | Process and kernel information |
| `/boot` | Boot-related files |
| `/opt` | Optional software |
| `/mnt` | Temporary mount points |
| `/media` | Removable media mount points |

### Example

```text
/
├── home
│   └── vboxuser
│       ├── Documents
│       ├── Downloads
│       └── Projects
│
├── etc
├── var
│   └── log
├── usr
├── tmp
├── boot
└── dev
```

**Remember:** `/` and `/root` are different.

- `/` = Root of the entire file system.
- `/root` = Home directory of the root user.

