## 18. Hard Links and Soft Links

Links allow multiple directory entries to refer to file data or another file path.

### Hard Link

A hard link is another directory entry referring to the same inode.

Create:

```bash
ln original.txt hardlink.txt
```

Characteristics:
- Shares the same inode.
- Data remains accessible through another hard link if one name is deleted.
- Normally cannot cross file systems.
- Directories are generally not hard-linked by ordinary users.

### Soft Link (Symbolic Link)

A soft link stores a path pointing to another file or directory.

Create:

```bash
ln -s original.txt softlink.txt
```

Characteristics:
- Has its own inode.
- Points to a path.
- Can cross file systems.
- Can become broken if the target is moved or deleted.

### Difference

| Hard Link | Soft Link |
|---|---|
| Same inode | Different inode |
| Refers to file data | Refers to a path |
| Usually cannot cross file systems | Can cross file systems |
| Survives deletion of another hard-link name | May become broken if target disappears |

